---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Take part without learning ontology code" description="A practical route for property professionals to review definitions, diagrams, examples and rules through the website and working groups without learning semantic modelling languages.">
  <h1>Your expertise is the starting point</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    You do not need to learn ontology engineering to help build the model. You need to
    recognise the language, decisions and exceptions in your work. Read a definition,
    follow a diagram, explain what is missing, or bring an example that challenges an
    assumption. Facilitators handle the formal representation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="start-small">Start with something you know</h2>
  <p>
    Choose a familiar subject in <a href="/search">site search</a> or browse the
    <a href="/development/property-pack">Property Pack candidate</a>. You might start
    with an address, an inspection, a title or a question your team asks repeatedly.
    Read the page's definition and status before looking at its technical details.
    A candidate label means there is something to examine, not a conclusion you are
    expected to endorse.
  </p>
  <p>
    The website provides generated resource pages, diagrams, dictionaries, controlled
    values and validation information. These are different views of the proposed
    model. You can contribute by discussing the plain-language view alone. Downloading
    files, writing queries and understanding technical abbreviations are optional.
  </p>

  <h2 id="review-questions">Ask practical questions</h2>
  <p>
    Imagine a page that uses the phrase “survey date”. You know that the date of an
    inspection can differ from the date a report is issued. That observation is already
    a useful modelling contribution. Explain who needs each date and give a fictional
    example where combining them would lead to a wrong conclusion. The facilitator can
    then propose separate definitions and relationships for review.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Would a colleague understand this term in the same way?</li>
    <li>What does the definition include, and what should it exclude?</li>
    <li>Does the diagram connect the right things, in the right direction?</li>
    <li>What happens when the usual case does not apply?</li>
    <li>What source, date or qualification is needed to interpret the information?</li>
    <li>Would another profession use this word differently?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A counterexample is often more useful than a general expression of agreement. If
    a draft assumes a buyer is always an individual, describing a company purchasing a
    property identifies a concrete gap. You do not need to decide which technical
    construct should fix it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="read-diagrams">Read a diagram as ordinary sentences</h2>
  <p>
    Start with one box and read its labelled connection to the next: a transaction
    concerns a property; a report describes an inspection; an organisation supplied a
    statement. Ask whether the sentence makes sense in your work. Diagrams deliberately
    leave out detail so you can discuss a small part of the model at a time.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can also ignore the diagram and use the definitions and examples beneath it.
    Both must express compatible meaning. If they appear to disagree, report the
    difference. The <a href="/semantic-modelling/reading-the-model">model-reading
    guide</a> explains the page conventions without requiring you to become a modeller.
  </p>

  <h2 id="send-feedback">Make feedback easy to act on</h2>
  <p>
    Include the page link, the wording or relationship you are questioning, and its
    practical consequence. Suggest replacement wording if you have it, but a clear
    problem is enough. Describe the context and any evidence that could help resolve it.
    One issue at a time is easier to discuss and trace than a broad request to rewrite
    an entire model.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      “This page appears to use the report date as the inspection date. In our work,
      an inspection on Monday can produce a report on Thursday. We need both dates
      because they answer different questions. Could the definition distinguish them?”
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Where the deployed page comments are enabled, authorised signed-in participants
    can leave contextual feedback beside the material. The private working-group Teams
    threads remain the group discussion route. Follow the
    <a href="/development/working-groups/member-guide/teams-and-discussions">discussion
    guidance</a> for access and the appropriate channel.
  </p>

  <h2 id="bring-evidence">Bring existing knowledge, safely</h2>
  <p>
    Useful evidence includes an approved blank form, a glossary, published guidance
    your organisation may share, a field definition, or a fictional scenario showing
    an exception. Explain where it came from, which version it is, why it matters and
    what restrictions apply. A familiar document is evidence to interpret, not an
    instruction to reproduce its structure unchanged.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use the group's authorised intake route for files. Keep customer information and
    confidential material out of public comments and examples. The
    <a href="/development/working-groups/member-guide/source-material-and-sharepoint">source-material
    guide</a> explains accepted submissions and the private organisation areas.
  </p>

  <h2 id="after-review">Know what happens to a contribution</h2>
  <p>
    Facilitators interpret the evidence, propose changes and use technical checks to
    find inconsistencies. Practitioners then judge whether the revised meaning fits.
    Feedback can also expose an unresolved question or a difference between contexts;
    it does not have to produce an immediate merger or agreement.
  </p>
  <p>
    The intended wider workflow includes clearer change views and expanded discussion
    and review facilities. Those plans should not be mistaken for a fully operational
    end-to-end approval system. A comment, reaction or successful technical check does
    not adopt a standard. Each candidate and group records its own standing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/join">Express interest in a working group</a>, or use the
    <a href="/development/working-groups/member-guide">member guide</a> if you already
    participate. Your contribution can begin with one well-explained example.
  </p>
</Layout>
